// Requires map type support, and specifically a release in which map values
// that are a type imported from another namespace serialize correctly: 3.x
// accepts a map type only behind the enableMapType flag that the models build
// does not set, and the cross-namespace map value fix landed in 4.2.0
// (accordproject/concerto#1279). The requirement first held at 4.2.0; the
// declared floor tracks the release the models build supports, which is
// Concerto 5 since #204.
concerto version "^5.0.0"

namespace org.accordproject.runtime@1.0.0

import org.accordproject.agreement@1.0.0.Agreement from https://models.accordproject.org/accordproject/agreement@1.0.0.cto
import org.accordproject.crypto@1.0.0.ContentHash from https://models.accordproject.org/crypto/crypto@1.0.0.cto
import org.accordproject.templatedata@1.0.0.StateData from https://models.accordproject.org/accordproject/templatedata@1.0.0.cto

/**
 * Base request for invoking contract logic with no custom request properties.
 */
transaction Request {
}

/**
 * Base response for contract logic with no custom response properties.
 */
transaction Response {
}

/**
 * The state of an agreement's template instances, keyed by instance id.
 *
 * Keys are a document's AgreementDocument.documentId or a composed clause's
 * Clause.clauseId from agreement@1.0.0. Ids are stable across revisions, so
 * the index never has to mirror the shape of the agreement's tree, and a
 * stateless instance has no entry.
 *
 * The value is StateData itself, so a template's own state subtype is the map
 * value with nothing wrapping it. Both the declared value type and the
 * concrete type an instance carries are therefore imported from other
 * namespaces, which requires concerto-core 4.2.0 or later
 * (accordproject/concerto#1279).
 */
map InstanceStates {
  o String
  o StateData
}

/**
 * An identified, revisioned snapshot of the whole runtime state of an
 * agreement, including the state of each of its clause instances.
 *
 * AgreementState is registry-resident, and intentionally so. It holds a
 * relationship to its Agreement rather than the portable AgreementReference
 * used by obligations and settlement evidence, because state is written by a
 * runtime that has the agreement and its documents in hand: a revision counter
 * and a previous-state hash chain are only meaningful where successive
 * revisions can be resolved against one another. Records which must travel
 * standalone reference an agreement portably instead; state does not travel,
 * so it does not need to. This mirrors the distinction party@1.0.0 draws
 * between Party and PartyRef.
 *
 * State is held as one document per agreement rather than one asset per
 * clause. A transition which spans scopes, such as marking a clause BREACHED
 * because of a condition detected in the agreement or in a peer clause, is
 * then a single write producing a single revision and a single hash, and
 * sibling state is in scope without a further lookup. Per-clause state assets
 * would require a cross-asset transaction that Concerto does not provide, and
 * would leave revision and previousStateHash without a well-defined meaning.
 *
 * states holds every stateful instance's state, the documents' and the
 * composed clauses' alike, keyed by instance id. A template's logic reads and
 * writes only its own entry; a transition that changes a document and the
 * clauses composed into it is still one write and one revision.
 *
 * stateHash commits to the state, excluding the stateHash property itself,
 * and previousStateHash links a revision to its immediate predecessor when one
 * exists. Both are optional and this model does not define the canonicalization
 * they use, so a hash chain over revisions is only meaningful under a profile
 * that fixes those rules. A deterministic profile must define the canonical
 * serialization, state that stateHash is excluded from its own pre-image, and
 * should require stateHash where the chain is relied upon. Where a hash is
 * supplied its crypto@1.0.0 Canonicalization should be populated. The
 * properties are optional rather than required because optional to required is
 * a breaking change and this namespace is published permanently.
 *
 * Obligations are intentionally not defined in this namespace. New
 * implementations should use org.accordproject.obligation@1.0.0 so durable
 * obligation state and lifecycle events are not coupled to the runtime API.
 */
asset AgreementState identified by stateId {
  o String stateId
  --> Agreement agreement
  o Long revision default=0 range=[0,]
  o DateTime effectiveAt
  o InstanceStates states optional
  o ContentHash stateHash optional
  o ContentHash previousStateHash optional
}
